1
00:00:00,112 --> 00:00:01,460
You are the frightened young

2
00:00:01,460 --> 00:00:03,134
sailor who handed the quartermaster

3
00:00:03,134 --> 00:00:05,383
the ship's papers with both hands.

4
00:00:05,383 --> 00:00:06,465
You thought that was

5
00:00:06,465 --> 00:00:07,908
the worst day of your life.

6
00:00:07,908 --> 00:00:09,784
It was only the first one.

7
00:00:09,784 --> 00:00:10,903
So far in this series,

8
00:00:10,903 --> 00:00:12,383
you have been Tom,

9
00:00:12,383 --> 00:00:14,354
the captain, the quartermaster,

10
00:00:14,354 --> 00:00:18,664
the surgeon, the carpenter, the navigator and the gunner.

11
00:00:18,664 --> 00:00:20,526
Every one of them belonged

12
00:00:20,526 --> 00:00:22,646
to the crew, or was made to.

13
00:00:22,646 --> 00:00:23,948
This time,

14
00:00:23,948 --> 00:00:26,085
you are on the other side of the rail.

15
00:00:26,085 --> 00:00:27,974
You are what the crew takes.

16
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:30,115
Imagine you are a merchant

17
00:00:30,115 --> 00:00:30,989
sailor of nineteen,

18
00:00:30,989 --> 00:00:33,647
in the Caribbean around seventeen seventeen.

19
00:00:33,647 --> 00:00:34,916
You are thin,

20
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:39,526
with straight black hair, a first wispy mustache,

21
00:00:39,526 --> 00:00:42,783
a faded red-and-white striped shirt and

22
00:00:42,783 --> 00:00:44,930
canvas trousers rolled to the knee,

23
00:00:44,930 --> 00:00:47,912
and you work barefoot like most sailors.

24
00:00:47,912 --> 00:00:48,303
You,

25
00:00:48,303 --> 00:00:51,313
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

26
00:00:51,313 --> 00:00:54,293
The problems come from trial records,

27
00:00:54,293 --> 00:00:55,613
eyewitness accounts,

28
00:00:55,613 --> 00:00:57,373
a captive's own memoir and

29
00:00:57,373 --> 00:01:00,565
historians' research on real pirate crews.

30
00:01:00,565 --> 00:01:03,799
And remember who this story is about.

31
00:01:03,799 --> 00:01:04,904
Not the legend.

32
00:01:04,904 --> 00:01:06,930
The people the legend robbed.

33
00:01:06,930 --> 00:01:09,694
A few weeks after that first robbery,

34
00:01:09,694 --> 00:01:11,481
you sign on to a new sloop carrying

35
00:01:11,481 --> 00:01:13,962
flour and timber between the islands.

36
00:01:13,962 --> 00:01:14,901
The pay is poor,

37
00:01:14,901 --> 00:01:17,743
but at least the ship is still yours to sail.

38
00:01:18,132 --> 00:01:20,306
Then, on the third morning out,

39
00:01:20,306 --> 00:01:23,715
a familiar sail comes over the horizon.

40
00:01:23,715 --> 00:01:24,592
Same sloop.

41
00:01:24,592 --> 00:01:25,696
Same crew.

42
00:01:26,069 --> 00:01:27,795
The same quartermaster comes

43
00:01:27,795 --> 00:01:30,675
over the rail first, looks at you for

44
00:01:30,675 --> 00:01:32,979
a long moment, and says, "Again?"

45
00:01:32,979 --> 00:01:34,006
This time,

46
00:01:34,006 --> 00:01:36,339
they do not just take the cargo.

47
00:01:36,339 --> 00:01:38,913
Since they captured a second sloop,

48
00:01:38,913 --> 00:01:40,268
the crew has two vessels

49
00:01:40,268 --> 00:01:42,665
and not enough hands to sail them.

50
00:01:42,665 --> 00:01:44,406
The captain looks over your crew

51
00:01:44,406 --> 00:01:46,957
the way a buyer looks at barrels.

52
00:01:46,957 --> 00:01:48,084
He points at you.

53
00:01:48,084 --> 00:01:50,358
Real pirate crews filled their

54
00:01:50,358 --> 00:01:52,187
ranks from the ships they robbed.

55
00:01:52,187 --> 00:01:53,892
Over the last four episodes,

56
00:01:53,892 --> 00:01:56,480
we met men taken for their skills:

57
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:00,065
surgeons, carpenters, navigators and gunners.

58
00:02:00,065 --> 00:02:02,432
But ordinary sailors and

59
00:02:02,432 --> 00:02:04,723
fishermen were taken too.

60
00:02:04,723 --> 00:02:06,236
In June seventeen twenty-two,

61
00:02:06,614 --> 00:02:09,980
Edward Low's pirates captured a young fisherman from Marblehead,

62
00:02:09,980 --> 00:02:11,101
Massachusetts,

63
00:02:11,101 --> 00:02:14,941
named Philip Ashton, off the coast of Nova Scotia.

64
00:02:14,941 --> 00:02:16,543
His story survives because he

65
00:02:16,543 --> 00:02:17,931
told it himself,

66
00:02:17,931 --> 00:02:20,574
in a memoir published in seventeen twenty-five.

67
00:02:20,574 --> 00:02:22,978
According to Ashton's own account,

68
00:02:22,978 --> 00:02:25,861
Low asked the captives whether they were married.

69
00:02:26,186 --> 00:02:27,617
In the Boston court files,

70
00:02:27,617 --> 00:02:31,396
the pirates who kept the carpenter Thomas Davis in

71
00:02:31,396 --> 00:02:33,872
seventeen sixteen said they kept him

72
00:02:33,872 --> 00:02:36,392
because he was "a carpenter and a single man."

73
00:02:37,150 --> 00:02:38,554
A man with nobody waiting

74
00:02:38,554 --> 00:02:40,367
at home was easier to keep.

75
00:02:40,757 --> 00:02:43,156
The captain asks you the same question.

76
00:02:43,156 --> 00:02:45,486
You say yes, very quickly.

77
00:02:45,486 --> 00:02:48,411
You have never been married in your life.

78
00:02:49,042 --> 00:02:50,299
It does not help.

79
00:02:50,299 --> 00:02:52,754
You are taken across to the

80
00:02:52,754 --> 00:02:54,449
pirate sloop with nothing but the

81
00:02:54,449 --> 00:02:56,493
striped shirt you are wearing.

82
00:02:56,493 --> 00:02:57,913
From the flour sloop,

83
00:02:57,913 --> 00:03:01,810
an old gray-bearded shipmate raises one hand slowly,

84
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:04,699
the way people wave at a funeral.

85
00:03:04,699 --> 00:03:05,858
You wave back.

86
00:03:05,858 --> 00:03:07,973
Nobody stops either of you.

87
00:03:07,973 --> 00:03:09,585
Your first night aboard,

88
00:03:09,585 --> 00:03:12,161
Tom gives you half his biscuit.

89
00:03:12,161 --> 00:03:13,404
He tells you he came

90
00:03:13,404 --> 00:03:15,121
aboard of his own free will.

91
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:17,741
He says it as if it were a confession.

92
00:03:17,741 --> 00:03:18,947
You notice he does not

93
00:03:18,947 --> 00:03:20,969
look at you while he says it.

94
00:03:20,969 --> 00:03:21,871
The next evening,

95
00:03:21,871 --> 00:03:24,770
the quartermaster brings out the articles and a pen.

96
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:25,877
You have heard what

97
00:03:25,877 --> 00:03:28,001
happened to men who refused.

98
00:03:28,001 --> 00:03:30,276
Ashton refused to join Low's crew.

99
00:03:30,779 --> 00:03:32,706
In his memoir, he wrote that Low

100
00:03:32,706 --> 00:03:35,757
"cocked his pistol and clapped it to my head."

101
00:03:35,757 --> 00:03:37,740
He was threatened again and again,

102
00:03:37,740 --> 00:03:39,795
especially by Low's quartermaster,

103
00:03:39,795 --> 00:03:40,626
John Russell,

104
00:03:40,626 --> 00:03:43,907
who by Ashton's account once tried to shoot him with a

105
00:03:43,907 --> 00:03:46,779
pistol that misfired, again and again.

106
00:03:46,779 --> 00:03:48,364
Ashton still refused.

107
00:03:48,364 --> 00:03:51,173
At the Cape Coast Castle trial of

108
00:03:51,173 --> 00:03:53,799
Bartholomew Roberts's crews in seventeen twenty-two,

109
00:03:54,220 --> 00:03:55,036
prisoner after

110
00:03:55,036 --> 00:03:57,849
prisoner said he had been forced to sign.

111
00:03:57,849 --> 00:03:58,906
Some described being

112
00:03:58,906 --> 00:04:00,766
threatened by Roberts himself.

113
00:04:01,366 --> 00:04:03,364
And here is the cruel part.

114
00:04:03,364 --> 00:04:06,854
Captain Charles Johnson's version of that trial,

115
00:04:06,854 --> 00:04:07,988
printed in his General

116
00:04:07,988 --> 00:04:09,923
History of the Pyrates in seventeen twenty-four,

117
00:04:09,923 --> 00:04:12,874
records the court's suspicion.

118
00:04:13,249 --> 00:04:14,938
A pistol and the articles,

119
00:04:14,938 --> 00:04:18,259
"served up in a dish together," the court observed,

120
00:04:18,259 --> 00:04:20,498
was often a performance arranged between

121
00:04:20,498 --> 00:04:22,383
the pirates and the new man,

122
00:04:22,383 --> 00:04:25,516
so that he would look forced if he were ever caught.

123
00:04:25,516 --> 00:04:26,798
Johnson is a pen name,

124
00:04:26,798 --> 00:04:29,327
and his book mixes fact with invention,

125
00:04:29,700 --> 00:04:31,521
but the trial record shows the court

126
00:04:31,521 --> 00:04:33,495
weighing that doubt case by case.

127
00:04:33,884 --> 00:04:36,119
So even a pistol to your head might not

128
00:04:36,119 --> 00:04:38,413
be enough to prove that you were forced.

129
00:04:38,413 --> 00:04:41,162
The court would ask whether you meant it.

130
00:04:41,162 --> 00:04:42,416
You do not sign.

131
00:04:42,416 --> 00:04:43,891
The captain shrugs.

132
00:04:43,891 --> 00:04:45,250
The quartermaster

133
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:46,967
writes something in his book.

134
00:04:46,967 --> 00:04:47,540
For once,

135
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:50,019
you are certain what the word is,

136
00:04:50,019 --> 00:04:52,942
because you watch him write it: "forced."

137
00:04:53,413 --> 00:04:54,785
It is the most important word

138
00:04:54,785 --> 00:04:57,052
anyone has written about you in your life.

139
00:04:57,523 --> 00:04:59,084
Beside it, he adds the date

140
00:04:59,084 --> 00:05:01,441
and the name of your flour sloop.

141
00:05:01,441 --> 00:05:02,843
Nobody has ever bothered to

142
00:05:02,843 --> 00:05:04,965
write down where you came from before.

143
00:05:04,965 --> 00:05:07,191
Forced men knew they would need proof,

144
00:05:07,191 --> 00:05:09,687
and they looked for it everywhere.

145
00:05:09,687 --> 00:05:10,542
At Cape Coast,

146
00:05:10,542 --> 00:05:13,735
two musicians from the ship Cornwall said they had

147
00:05:13,735 --> 00:05:15,930
given all their wages to their own captain,

148
00:05:15,930 --> 00:05:17,192
Captain Rolls,

149
00:05:17,192 --> 00:05:20,508
begging him to put them in the Gazette as forced men.

150
00:05:21,122 --> 00:05:23,257
A newspaper notice that a man had been

151
00:05:23,257 --> 00:05:25,721
taken by force could be evidence later.

152
00:05:25,721 --> 00:05:27,967
Both musicians were acquitted.

153
00:05:28,421 --> 00:05:30,110
A witness said the eager surgeon

154
00:05:30,110 --> 00:05:32,431
Peter Scudamore had asked the same

155
00:05:32,431 --> 00:05:34,437
captain to put him in the Gazette.

156
00:05:34,437 --> 00:05:35,880
The captain refused,

157
00:05:35,880 --> 00:05:38,939
and the witness believed no force had been used on him at all.

158
00:05:39,425 --> 00:05:40,817
Scudamore was hanged.

159
00:05:40,817 --> 00:05:42,273
Newspapers of the time

160
00:05:42,273 --> 00:05:44,460
were full of captives' stories.

161
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:46,546
Leeson notes that eighteenth-century

162
00:05:46,546 --> 00:05:49,163
papers published pirate victims' accounts,

163
00:05:49,163 --> 00:05:51,679
sometimes describing the pirates' flag,

164
00:05:51,679 --> 00:05:54,861
their numbers and even their captain's temper.

165
00:05:55,347 --> 00:05:56,629
For a family at home,

166
00:05:56,629 --> 00:05:58,611
a line of print might be the only

167
00:05:58,611 --> 00:06:01,106
sign that a son was still alive.

168
00:06:01,106 --> 00:06:03,073
Johnson's version of the trial

169
00:06:03,073 --> 00:06:06,034
explains why the court mistrusted those notices:

170
00:06:06,034 --> 00:06:08,284
they were meant "to answer the end of

171
00:06:08,284 --> 00:06:10,977
being put in a newspaper or affidavit."

172
00:06:10,977 --> 00:06:12,286
The proof could be faked.

173
00:06:12,286 --> 00:06:13,495
So the court looked for

174
00:06:13,495 --> 00:06:16,451
something harder to fake: witnesses.

175
00:06:16,451 --> 00:06:19,517
Months ago, a ship's surgeon taken from

176
00:06:19,517 --> 00:06:22,146
your old sloop asked you to tell anyone

177
00:06:22,146 --> 00:06:25,139
who asked that he had been taken by force.

178
00:06:25,139 --> 00:06:26,659
You nodded so hard he

179
00:06:26,659 --> 00:06:28,584
was not sure you heard him.

180
00:06:28,584 --> 00:06:29,916
You heard him.

181
00:06:30,596 --> 00:06:31,775
Now you understand

182
00:06:31,775 --> 00:06:34,061
exactly what he was asking for.

183
00:06:34,532 --> 00:06:36,020
The merchant sloop you were

184
00:06:36,020 --> 00:06:38,286
taken from is about to sail away.

185
00:06:38,789 --> 00:06:40,326
You run to the rail and shout

186
00:06:40,326 --> 00:06:41,607
your name to her master,

187
00:06:41,607 --> 00:06:43,977
and the name of your mother's village.

188
00:06:43,977 --> 00:06:46,507
Tell them I was forced, you shout.

189
00:06:47,042 --> 00:06:47,651
He nods.

190
00:06:47,651 --> 00:06:48,803
You hope he is a

191
00:06:48,803 --> 00:06:51,191
better witness than you were.

192
00:06:51,191 --> 00:06:54,538
Life as a forced man is mostly work.

193
00:06:54,538 --> 00:06:55,391
You pump,

194
00:06:55,391 --> 00:06:59,287
haul, scrub and stand watch like everyone else.

195
00:06:59,287 --> 00:07:00,517
The carpenter shows

196
00:07:00,517 --> 00:07:02,528
you how to caulk a seam.

197
00:07:02,528 --> 00:07:05,290
The navigator lets you hold the sandglass.

198
00:07:05,679 --> 00:07:08,361
The gunner makes you sponge out a gun,

199
00:07:08,361 --> 00:07:10,874
and is very serious about it.

200
00:07:10,874 --> 00:07:13,052
But nobody quite trusts you.

201
00:07:13,618 --> 00:07:16,112
In Boston, in seventeen seventeen,

202
00:07:16,112 --> 00:07:19,263
a shipmate of the forced carpenter

203
00:07:19,263 --> 00:07:21,397
Thomas Davis testified that he kept his

204
00:07:21,397 --> 00:07:24,175
distance from Davis aboard Bellamy's ship,

205
00:07:24,175 --> 00:07:26,691
because the pirates suspected forced

206
00:07:26,691 --> 00:07:28,624
men of planning to run away together.

207
00:07:28,624 --> 00:07:30,247
And escape was dangerous.

208
00:07:30,686 --> 00:07:32,568
Johnson describes Phillips's crew

209
00:07:32,568 --> 00:07:34,797
killing men who tried to leave,

210
00:07:34,797 --> 00:07:37,410
and says those severities made it dangerous

211
00:07:37,410 --> 00:07:40,254
for anyone even to talk about escaping.

212
00:07:40,254 --> 00:07:42,587
A Boston deposition confirms

213
00:07:42,587 --> 00:07:43,996
that two of Phillips's men

214
00:07:43,996 --> 00:07:46,099
had been shot by their captain.

215
00:07:46,506 --> 00:07:47,816
One night at the tiller,

216
00:07:47,816 --> 00:07:50,320
the navigator tells you about Harry Glasby,

217
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:54,084
Roberts's forced master, who tried once to slip away

218
00:07:54,084 --> 00:07:56,664
and was never allowed off the ship again.

219
00:07:57,118 --> 00:07:58,408
He tells it quietly,

220
00:07:58,408 --> 00:08:00,673
as a warning and as a kindness.

221
00:08:01,063 --> 00:08:02,557
So you keep your head down,

222
00:08:02,557 --> 00:08:05,272
and you keep your eyes on the horizon.

223
00:08:05,272 --> 00:08:06,842
On a long night watch,

224
00:08:06,842 --> 00:08:09,370
Tom finally tells you why he joined.

225
00:08:09,370 --> 00:08:11,270
A man in a tavern described a

226
00:08:11,270 --> 00:08:12,767
share of captured wealth,

227
00:08:12,767 --> 00:08:15,540
and described it more carefully than the work.

228
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:19,205
Tom imagined gold and a magnificent coat.

229
00:08:19,205 --> 00:08:20,871
He got a bucket.

230
00:08:20,871 --> 00:08:23,310
He tells you this as if it were funny.

231
00:08:23,310 --> 00:08:24,486
Then he says,

232
00:08:24,843 --> 00:08:27,947
quietly, that at least he chose the bucket.

233
00:08:28,384 --> 00:08:30,483
You did not even get to choose that.

234
00:08:30,483 --> 00:08:33,095
You think about your mother's village,

235
00:08:33,095 --> 00:08:34,311
about the flour sloop,

236
00:08:34,311 --> 00:08:36,162
and about the merchant captain who

237
00:08:36,162 --> 00:08:38,625
promised to tell people your name.

238
00:08:38,625 --> 00:08:40,011
You wonder if anyone at

239
00:08:40,011 --> 00:08:42,006
home knows you are alive.

240
00:08:42,006 --> 00:08:44,223
Then the bell rings for the next watch,

241
00:08:44,223 --> 00:08:46,386
and you go back to the pump.

242
00:08:46,386 --> 00:08:47,628
Then comes the order

243
00:08:47,628 --> 00:08:49,048
you have been dreading.

244
00:08:49,048 --> 00:08:50,588
A sail appears,

245
00:08:50,588 --> 00:08:52,303
the chase ends in surrender,

246
00:08:52,303 --> 00:08:55,694
and the quartermaster puts a cutlass in your hand.

247
00:08:55,694 --> 00:08:57,566
"Boarding list," he says.

248
00:08:57,566 --> 00:08:58,724
"Your turn."

249
00:08:59,403 --> 00:09:01,557
You climb over the rail with the others.

250
00:09:01,557 --> 00:09:04,559
You hold the cutlass the way Tom holds a bucket,

251
00:09:04,559 --> 00:09:06,591
as far from your body as possible.

252
00:09:07,110 --> 00:09:08,681
You do not raise it.

253
00:09:08,681 --> 00:09:10,927
You do not take anything.

254
00:09:11,462 --> 00:09:13,433
But the merchant sailors staring at

255
00:09:13,433 --> 00:09:15,900
you do not know that you are forced.

256
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:16,640
To them,

257
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:20,103
you are just another pirate with a blade.

258
00:09:20,103 --> 00:09:22,407
This mattered more than any oath.

259
00:09:23,022 --> 00:09:24,643
In the quartermaster episode,

260
00:09:24,643 --> 00:09:26,908
we heard how witnesses in Boston said

261
00:09:26,908 --> 00:09:29,239
that John Rose Archer went armed

262
00:09:29,239 --> 00:09:31,945
aboard every vessel his crew took.

263
00:09:31,945 --> 00:09:34,883
At Cape Coast, Johnson's version of the

264
00:09:34,883 --> 00:09:37,014
trial puts the court's rule plainly:

265
00:09:37,014 --> 00:09:40,956
"it is not so material how a man comes among pirates,

266
00:09:40,956 --> 00:09:43,744
as how he acts when he is there."

267
00:09:43,744 --> 00:09:45,694
A forced man seen armed on a

268
00:09:45,694 --> 00:09:48,307
prize could be hanged as a volunteer.

269
00:09:48,307 --> 00:09:49,495
That night,

270
00:09:49,495 --> 00:09:52,694
you ask the quartermaster to write it in his book:

271
00:09:52,694 --> 00:09:55,534
armed by order, took nothing.

272
00:09:56,051 --> 00:09:58,722
He looks at you for a long time.

273
00:09:58,722 --> 00:10:00,372
Then he writes it.

274
00:10:00,372 --> 00:10:03,907
You do not know if that is kindness,

275
00:10:03,907 --> 00:10:06,358
or simply very good bookkeeping.

276
00:10:06,813 --> 00:10:08,288
Being a captive was not

277
00:10:08,288 --> 00:10:10,317
only about being forced to join.

278
00:10:10,317 --> 00:10:12,168
It was about watching everything

279
00:10:12,168 --> 00:10:14,467
that happened to the other captives.

280
00:10:14,986 --> 00:10:17,321
The economist Peter Leeson argues that

281
00:10:17,321 --> 00:10:19,644
pirates worked hard to build a

282
00:10:19,644 --> 00:10:22,602
reputation for punishing captives who hid valuables,

283
00:10:22,602 --> 00:10:25,088
and rewarding those who cooperated.

284
00:10:25,088 --> 00:10:27,900
At Cape Coast, a witness said that one

285
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:30,157
captured sailor had shown the pirates

286
00:10:30,157 --> 00:10:32,581
where his own ship's cook had hidden

287
00:10:32,581 --> 00:10:35,652
some rings and buckles, and had them taken.

288
00:10:35,652 --> 00:10:36,749
Captives could be

289
00:10:36,749 --> 00:10:38,874
turned against each other.

290
00:10:38,874 --> 00:10:40,497
You watch it happen on your

291
00:10:40,497 --> 00:10:42,661
first prize as a forced man.

292
00:10:42,661 --> 00:10:44,247
The pirates ask a frightened

293
00:10:44,247 --> 00:10:46,188
passenger where the money is.

294
00:10:46,563 --> 00:10:47,884
A sailor from his own ship

295
00:10:47,884 --> 00:10:50,140
points before the passenger can answer.

296
00:10:50,483 --> 00:10:52,348
The quartermaster takes the purse.

297
00:10:52,348 --> 00:10:54,130
Nobody looks at anybody.

298
00:10:54,130 --> 00:10:56,699
Some captives were held for weeks.

299
00:10:56,699 --> 00:10:58,365
William Snelgrave,

300
00:10:58,365 --> 00:11:00,838
the slave-trading captain taken by pirates at the

301
00:11:00,838 --> 00:11:02,660
Sierra Leone River in seventeen nineteen,

302
00:11:02,660 --> 00:11:06,720
wrote that by the time the pirates sailed away,

303
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:08,760
he had been in their hands a month,

304
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:10,603
"and many others much longer."

305
00:11:10,603 --> 00:11:11,889
Some captives were

306
00:11:11,889 --> 00:11:14,015
held as bargaining pieces.

307
00:11:14,452 --> 00:11:17,766
When Blackbeard blockaded Charleston in seventeen eighteen,

308
00:11:18,074 --> 00:11:18,875
Royal Museums

309
00:11:18,875 --> 00:11:21,452
Greenwich says he took the crews of ships as

310
00:11:21,452 --> 00:11:23,530
hostages and threatened to kill them

311
00:11:23,530 --> 00:11:26,033
unless the town paid his ransom of medicine.

312
00:11:26,486 --> 00:11:27,816
And some captives never had

313
00:11:27,816 --> 00:11:29,542
any hope of a hearing at all.

314
00:11:30,251 --> 00:11:32,533
After Roberts's crews were captured

315
00:11:32,533 --> 00:11:33,404
in seventeen twenty-two,

316
00:11:33,776 --> 00:11:37,302
the historian David Wilson writes that the remaining captives,

317
00:11:37,302 --> 00:11:38,951
described only as "black

318
00:11:38,951 --> 00:11:41,868
men," were sold into slavery without trial.

319
00:11:41,868 --> 00:11:43,887
The Canadian Encyclopedia puts

320
00:11:43,887 --> 00:11:45,720
their number at seventy-seven.

321
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:47,173
The Whydah's pilot,

322
00:11:47,173 --> 00:11:50,604
John Julian, survived the wreck of seventeen seventeen.

323
00:11:50,604 --> 00:11:53,345
Court records describe

324
00:11:53,345 --> 00:11:55,093
him as Native American.

325
00:11:55,093 --> 00:11:58,036
He was never tried, and History Is Now

326
00:11:58,036 --> 00:12:00,489
magazine says he was sold into slavery.

327
00:12:01,007 --> 00:12:01,585
For them,

328
00:12:01,585 --> 00:12:04,081
there was no newspaper notice to buy,

329
00:12:04,534 --> 00:12:07,865
no captain to plead, no court to persuade.

330
00:12:08,238 --> 00:12:09,399
The law did not ask

331
00:12:09,399 --> 00:12:11,440
whether they had been forced.

332
00:12:11,830 --> 00:12:14,075
It treated them as property.

333
00:12:14,075 --> 00:12:15,304
Weeks pass.

334
00:12:15,854 --> 00:12:18,386
Your crew anchors off a quiet island to

335
00:12:18,386 --> 00:12:20,539
take on water, and you are sent ashore

336
00:12:20,539 --> 00:12:22,845
with the water barrels for the first time.

337
00:12:23,460 --> 00:12:25,939
The trees come right down to the beach.

338
00:12:25,939 --> 00:12:27,792
You think about running.

339
00:12:27,792 --> 00:12:29,909
Philip Ashton did run.

340
00:12:30,862 --> 00:12:32,548
In March seventeen twenty-three,

341
00:12:32,548 --> 00:12:35,642
after about nine months as Low's captive,

342
00:12:35,642 --> 00:12:38,517
he went ashore with a watering party at Roatán,

343
00:12:38,517 --> 00:12:41,502
an island off the coast of Central America.

344
00:12:42,021 --> 00:12:43,826
He slipped into the woods and hid until

345
00:12:43,826 --> 00:12:46,794
the pirates gave up looking and sailed away.

346
00:12:46,794 --> 00:12:47,682
He was free,

347
00:12:47,682 --> 00:12:50,285
and for a long time completely alone.

348
00:12:50,285 --> 00:12:53,479
He survived on the island for about sixteen months,

349
00:12:53,479 --> 00:12:54,329
through heat,

350
00:12:54,329 --> 00:12:58,029
insects and hunger, with no tools to hunt or fish.

351
00:12:58,029 --> 00:12:59,923
He was finally carried home

352
00:12:59,923 --> 00:13:01,556
aboard a ship to Salem,

353
00:13:01,556 --> 00:13:04,324
and reached Massachusetts in seventeen twenty-five.

354
00:13:04,795 --> 00:13:05,651
That same year,

355
00:13:05,651 --> 00:13:09,489
his minister, John Barnard, published his story in Boston.

356
00:13:09,489 --> 00:13:12,039
The title is longer than most chapters:

357
00:13:12,477 --> 00:13:13,620
Ashton's Memorial,

358
00:13:13,620 --> 00:13:15,846
an History of the Strange Adventures,

359
00:13:15,846 --> 00:13:17,836
and Signal Deliverances, of Mr.

360
00:13:17,836 --> 00:13:19,099
Philip Ashton.

361
00:13:19,617 --> 00:13:21,494
You look at the trees for a long time.

362
00:13:21,494 --> 00:13:23,281
Then you look at your bare feet.

363
00:13:23,281 --> 00:13:25,625
Then you think about sixteen months.

364
00:13:25,625 --> 00:13:26,941
You carry the water

365
00:13:26,941 --> 00:13:28,419
barrels back to the boat.

366
00:13:28,419 --> 00:13:30,368
You are not proud of it.

367
00:13:30,368 --> 00:13:31,267
You are alive.

368
00:13:31,267 --> 00:13:34,086
Other captives found a different way out.

369
00:13:34,556 --> 00:13:35,759
In April seventeen twenty-four,

370
00:13:35,759 --> 00:13:39,046
the men held prisoner aboard John Phillips's

371
00:13:39,046 --> 00:13:41,377
pirate sloop rose up together,

372
00:13:41,377 --> 00:13:44,572
led by captured men like the fisherman John Fillmore

373
00:13:44,572 --> 00:13:47,016
and the forced carpenter Edward Cheeseman,

374
00:13:47,016 --> 00:13:49,633
and overpowered the crew within minutes.

375
00:13:49,633 --> 00:13:51,247
In Boston that May,

376
00:13:51,247 --> 00:13:53,891
the court acquitted all seven of the men who

377
00:13:53,891 --> 00:13:55,614
had planned the rising together.

378
00:13:55,988 --> 00:13:57,217
Their own testimony,

379
00:13:57,217 --> 00:13:59,917
and each other's, proved what they were.

380
00:14:00,468 --> 00:14:02,587
Fillmore later said the court even gave

381
00:14:02,587 --> 00:14:05,847
him the dead pirate captain's sword and gun.

382
00:14:05,847 --> 00:14:07,953
Witnesses were everything.

383
00:14:07,953 --> 00:14:10,007
At Cape Coast, more than seventy of

384
00:14:10,007 --> 00:14:12,430
Roberts's men were acquitted,

385
00:14:12,430 --> 00:14:14,863
most of them men who had been forced aboard,

386
00:14:14,863 --> 00:14:18,305
often because somebody stood up and said so.

387
00:14:18,305 --> 00:14:20,180
Fifty-two were hanged.

388
00:14:20,715 --> 00:14:22,390
Then the dealer brings the news

389
00:14:22,390 --> 00:14:23,595
you have heard before:

390
00:14:23,595 --> 00:14:25,758
the king's pardon of seventeen seventeen.

391
00:14:25,758 --> 00:14:30,302
Surrender to a governor by the fifth of September,

392
00:14:30,302 --> 00:14:30,733
seventeen eighteen,

393
00:14:30,733 --> 00:14:33,559
and earlier piracies would be forgiven.

394
00:14:33,559 --> 00:14:36,441
You never signed, so you have no vote.

395
00:14:36,441 --> 00:14:37,808
You listen to the arguments

396
00:14:37,808 --> 00:14:39,914
from the hatch, holding your breath.

397
00:14:40,224 --> 00:14:41,844
The pardon wins, narrowly.

398
00:14:41,844 --> 00:14:44,479
You discover that you are crying,

399
00:14:44,479 --> 00:14:46,961
and so, for some reason, is Tom.

400
00:14:46,961 --> 00:14:49,282
The sloop sails into a colonial

401
00:14:49,282 --> 00:14:50,980
harbor under a white flag,

402
00:14:50,980 --> 00:14:54,591
past a stone fort with its guns run out, just in case.

403
00:14:54,591 --> 00:14:56,179
Soldiers line the quay.

404
00:14:56,586 --> 00:14:58,181
The whole crew is marched to a

405
00:14:58,181 --> 00:15:00,039
long table under an awning,

406
00:15:00,039 --> 00:15:02,438
where a clerk in a wig sits with a ledger

407
00:15:02,438 --> 00:15:04,633
even bigger than the quartermaster's.

408
00:15:04,633 --> 00:15:06,320
Somewhere behind you,

409
00:15:06,320 --> 00:15:08,283
the gunner mutters that the fort's guns

410
00:15:08,283 --> 00:15:10,105
are pointed at the wrong ship.

411
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:12,707
The clerk takes down every name.

412
00:15:12,707 --> 00:15:14,642
He reaches yours and asks the

413
00:15:14,642 --> 00:15:16,749
question you have rehearsed for months.

414
00:15:16,749 --> 00:15:18,313
Did you come willingly?

415
00:15:18,913 --> 00:15:21,415
You do not have a newspaper notice.

416
00:15:21,415 --> 00:15:23,405
You do not know whether the

417
00:15:23,405 --> 00:15:25,498
merchant captain ever told anyone.

418
00:15:25,498 --> 00:15:27,382
But you have three things.

419
00:15:27,382 --> 00:15:29,087
The quartermaster opens his

420
00:15:29,087 --> 00:15:31,406
book and shows the clerk the

421
00:15:31,406 --> 00:15:33,524
word next to your name: "forced."

422
00:15:33,524 --> 00:15:35,651
The surgeon in his leather apron

423
00:15:35,651 --> 00:15:38,210
steps forward and says he saw you taken.

424
00:15:38,647 --> 00:15:40,970
You once promised to speak for him.

425
00:15:40,970 --> 00:15:43,430
Today, he speaks for you.

426
00:15:43,430 --> 00:15:45,869
And Tom, who has never once said the

427
00:15:45,869 --> 00:15:48,682
right thing at the right time, says it now.

428
00:15:49,248 --> 00:15:51,038
"He never wanted to be here.

429
00:15:51,038 --> 00:15:53,127
I gave him half my biscuit."

430
00:15:53,662 --> 00:15:55,425
The clerk writes something down.

431
00:15:55,425 --> 00:15:57,890
You very much hope the word is "forced."

432
00:15:57,890 --> 00:15:58,996
A week later,

433
00:15:58,996 --> 00:16:02,439
a small trading boat drops you at the jetty

434
00:16:02,439 --> 00:16:03,823
below your mother's village.

435
00:16:03,823 --> 00:16:05,704
You walk up the path barefoot,

436
00:16:05,704 --> 00:16:07,361
in the same striped shirt,

437
00:16:07,361 --> 00:16:10,227
now mended twice by a pirate carpenter.

438
00:16:10,227 --> 00:16:11,722
Your mother is at the door.

439
00:16:11,722 --> 00:16:13,568
She has heard nothing at all.

440
00:16:13,568 --> 00:16:16,077
No newspaper ever printed your name.

441
00:16:16,077 --> 00:16:18,446
The merchant captain never came.

442
00:16:18,446 --> 00:16:19,093
For her,

443
00:16:19,093 --> 00:16:23,754
you were simply gone, and now you are simply back.

444
00:16:23,754 --> 00:16:25,962
You do not tell her everything.

445
00:16:25,962 --> 00:16:28,576
You tell her about Tom's biscuit.

446
00:16:28,576 --> 00:16:29,945
So why was being a pirate

447
00:16:29,945 --> 00:16:31,825
captive worse than you think?

448
00:16:32,166 --> 00:16:33,768
Because the crew needed your hands,

449
00:16:33,768 --> 00:16:35,991
and asked whether anybody would miss you.

450
00:16:36,557 --> 00:16:38,056
Because even a pistol to your

451
00:16:38,056 --> 00:16:40,483
head might not prove you were forced.

452
00:16:40,483 --> 00:16:42,874
Because escape could mean a bullet,

453
00:16:42,874 --> 00:16:45,017
or sixteen months alone on an island.

454
00:16:45,017 --> 00:16:46,827
And because, for the people the

455
00:16:46,827 --> 00:16:48,972
law already treated as property,

456
00:16:48,972 --> 00:16:51,075
there was no notice to buy and

457
00:16:51,075 --> 00:16:52,978
no witness who could save them.

458
00:16:52,978 --> 00:16:55,003
The legend calls it freedom.

459
00:16:55,329 --> 00:16:56,164
For a captive,

460
00:16:56,164 --> 00:16:58,856
piracy was something that happened to you.

461
00:16:59,214 --> 00:17:00,613
If you want another life

462
00:17:00,613 --> 00:17:01,732
behind the legends,

463
00:17:01,732 --> 00:17:05,309
told from both sides of the rail, subscribe.

464
00:17:05,309 --> 00:17:07,053
This is Pirate Pages.

465
00:17:07,053 --> 00:17:09,198
The lives behind the legends.
